---
title: Constraints & patterns
summary: What crosses the island boundary and what does not, with each rule paired with the mistake it prevents and the fix.
related:
  - regions/client-islands
  - data-state/state-context
  - reference/api
---

An island is a real boundary, and some things cross it while others quietly don't. Each rule below states the constraint, the mistake people usually run into first, and the way around it.

## Props cross. Children and snippets do not.

Props are serialized with devalue and cross into the island. Host children, snippets and callbacks **do not**, because they live in the host's scope rather than the island's, so you cannot give an island a snippet or a function from the page.

```svelte
<!-- ✗ the island never sees this snippet or callback -->
<Island onselect={handleSelect}>
  {#snippet row(item)}<Row {item} />{/snippet}
</Island>
```

Model the interaction with props and remote functions, or move the UI into the island's own `.svelte` file.

The one reserved exception is `ogygiaFallback` on a [server island](/docs/regions/server-islands), which that page describes.

## Captured host state is a snapshot

A value from the host that you read into island markup is a serialized snapshot rather than live state, so mutating it updates nothing.

```svelte
<!-- ✗ writes to a serialized snapshot; nothing updates -->
<button onclick={() => count++}>{count}</button>
```

```svelte
<!-- ✓ own the state inside the island -->
<script>
  let count = $state(props.initial);
</script>
<button onclick={() => count++}>{count}</button>
```

ogygia catches this mistake in two ways:

| Guard       | When    | What                                                     |
| ----------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| Build error | compile | captured-variable writes in island markup fail the build |
| Dev proxy   | runtime | a deep proxy throws on mutation (dev only)               |

Pass data in as props, and keep mutable state inside the island.

### Store reads are snapshots too

A `$store` auto-subscription read inside a snippet that crosses an island boundary is captured the same way. The compiler evaluates the subscription in the host, where the `$` syntax is legal, passes the **value** across as a plain serialized prop, and rewrites the snippet's copy to use it. The build warns and traces each such read. In ordinary Svelte `$store` is live, but across the boundary it is a snapshot frozen at render time, and later store updates never reach the island.

```svelte
{#snippet children()}
  <!-- captured as the VALUE of $country at render time (build warning points here) -->
  <span>{$country}</span>
{/snippet}
```

To make the snapshot explicit (and silence the warning), read the store in the host scope and use the plain value inside the snippet. The store **object** itself cannot cross, because the functions of a store declared in the host do not serialize, and the compiler warns about that too. For live state shared across the boundary, use a [wired class](/docs/data-state/state-context).

## Page-level lifecycle is dead code

On a `csr = false` page the page module never runs on the client, so top-level `onMount`, effects and event listeners in the page do nothing.

```svelte
<!-- ✗ in a csr = false page, this never fires -->
<script>
  onMount(() => startPolling());
</script>
```

When you reach for `onMount`, what you actually want is an island, so move everything that has to run in the browser into one. For initialization, telemetry or listeners that render no UI, use a [boot island](/docs/regions/client-islands#the-boot-island-run-code-not-ui), a headless component whose only job is its `$effect`.

Inline `<script>` tags in page markup are a related trap. They run **once**, on the first load, and not on each navigation, because the router swaps `<body>` without executing them. Code that should run per navigation belongs in an island; [the router](/docs/app/spa-router) explains why.

## Reach for an island, not a bare dynamic import

If you find yourself hand-rolling `import()` plus `mount` to lazy-load a component, use an island instead.

```ts
// ✗ hand-rolled lazy mount: no SSR HTML, no signed boundary
const { default: Chart } = await import('./Chart.svelte');
mount(Chart, { target, props });
```

`wake: 'idle'` and `wake: 'visible'` already do exactly that, with the SSR HTML in place first and a signed boundary around it.

## Choose the boundary before you build

A subtree that needs interactivity is an island, and one that doesn't is a [lake](/docs/regions/lakes) or plain page HTML. Wrapping static content in an island "to be safe" only ships JS you didn't need, so decide up front:

| Need                   | Boundary                                      |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Interactive            | island                                        |
| Static but heavy       | [lake](/docs/regions/lakes)                   |
| Request-dependent HTML | [server island](/docs/regions/server-islands) |

Retrofitting the boundary later is the expensive path.

## Dev is not prod

Two behaviours differ in dev, and both can hide defects until you ship:

- SSR query seeding falls back to a client refetch.
- The mutation-guard proxy runs only in dev.

Something that works in `dev` can therefore behave differently in production, so check data flows against a production build before you ship.
